// The packet schedule: how apps/pbrt/render.bonsai runs by default, and the
// one its numbers are reported with. A schedule is compiled alongside the
// program it schedules, as a second input:
//
//     ./build/compiler -p ssa --no-heap --ffp-contract \
//         -i apps/pbrt/render.bonsai -i apps/pbrt/schedules/packet.bonsai ...
//
// It is a file of its own so that the same program can be run another way by
// naming another file -- scalar.bonsai and perlane.bonsai beside this one --
// with nothing in the program changed; that is what makes the three
// comparable, and it is what the scripts' `--schedule` switches between. The
// decisions every way of running the program shares -- what the scene is
// stored as, how a node is packed -- stay with the program, in the `schedule`
// block at the end of render.bonsai. What is here is the transforms, and
// above all their order, which is the whole of the difference between the
// three files.
schedule {
    // The random walk is a tail recursion, so this turns it into a loop. Said
    // here rather than written as one: what the source says is that a path's
    // next step depends on its last, and how that is run -- a loop per pixel
    // now, a queue of suspended paths advanced together later -- is this
    // block's to decide.
    walk_step.loopify();
    path_step.loopify();
    full_path_step.loopify();
    // volpath's walks are tail recursions the same way -- the path's step,
    // the majorant walk inside a medium, the shadow ray's walk through
    // interfaces with its ratio-tracking steps -- and become loops the same
    // way. Left recursive, the gang below would have to be made of a
    // recursion, which vectorize() does not do.
    vol_path_step.loopify();
    vol_medium_step.loopify();
    vol_shadow_step.loopify();
    vol_shadow_track.loopify();

    // The samples of a pixel are a gang on the SIMD unit: ISPC-style, one lane
    // per sample, the whole path trace run a register's worth at a time --
    // sixteen 32-bit lanes, the machine's AVX-512 register. `spp` is a
    // runtime count, and vectorize wants a constant gang width, so split
    // peels a width-sixteen inner loop off it first and that is what is
    // vectorized. The `true` asks for the split's tail: a scene whose spp is
    // not a multiple of sixteen runs its last gang partly full, the guard the
    // tail puts in front of the body being that gang's mask. Without it the
    // split runs whole gangs regardless, and a scene at four samples a pixel
    // silently got sixteen -- the same mean, a quarter of the noise, and a
    // render that took four times as long as it should have.
    render.split(s, s_gang, s_lane, 16, true)
          .vectorize(s_lane);

    // The gang is made before the traversals below are put on their stacks,
    // and the order is the point. A traversal loopified first is a loop each
    // lane walks at its own pace over a stack of its own: sixteen nodes
    // gathered at every step, sixteen samples of one pixel fetching one
    // address sixteen times (that is perlane.bonsai). Vectorized first, the
    // recursion is made by the gang as a whole -- one node the lanes share, a
    // mask of the lanes whose ray reached it -- and that is what loopify()
    // then puts on a stack: one of nodes and one of masks, each node's bounds
    // and children loaded once and broadcast, only the ray tests per lane.
    // That is a packet traversal (Wald, Slusallek, Benthin & Wagner 2001),
    // written as the composition of two directives. The sort's order is then
    // one decision for the gang, by a vote of the lanes' keys (see
    // SSA/SortRecursion.h).
    //
    // pbrt: `if (dirIsNeg[node->axis])`, which is the whole of how
    // BVHAggregate::Intersect decides which child to descend into first.
    //
    //     int axis = node->axis;
    //     if (dirIsNeg[axis]) { visit the second child first }
    //
    // and `dirIsNeg[i]` is `invDir[i] < 0` for `invDir = 1 / ray.d`, which is
    // what the key spells out. The reciprocal is kept even though `d[axis] < 0`
    // decides the same way everywhere except a component of exactly -0: this
    // renderer's job is to compute what pbrt computes, and where the two differ
    // it should differ for a reason better than a divide.
    //
    // Visiting the near child first is what makes the running best distance
    // tighten early, so every subtree whose nearest point is already further
    // than that is skipped. It changes no answer -- an `argmin` over a set does
    // not depend on the order the set is visited in -- so it belongs here and
    // not in `trace`, which says only which primitive is nearest.
    //
    // The key is the child index, flipped when the ray runs backwards along the
    // axis the node was split on. `axis` is a field of the Interior arm and was
    // carried by the layout from the beginning without anything reading it.
    //
    // The same rule inside an instance's tree, where pbrt applies it to the
    // ray *after* `ApplyInverse`: the split axis is an axis of the instance's
    // frame. The key is written over the query's object here too -- the ray
    // with its bound, `RaySegment`, of which the direction is what the key
    // reads -- and the compiler carries it into the pulled-back one along
    // with every other test in that walk (Opt/PullQueries.h).
    trace.sort(primitives.Interior,
               |i : u8, q : RaySegment, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / q.ray.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .sort(Inst.blas.Interior,
               |i : u8, q : RaySegment, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / q.ray.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .loopify(64);
    // The occlusion test gets its own stack, and it is the same 64 pbrt's
    // IntersectP uses.
    trace_any.loopify(64);

    // Which arms the gang skips. A vectorized `match` or `if` computes every
    // arm under a mask of the lanes in it; an arm no lane is in is paid for
    // unless a uniform `any(mask)` test is put in front of it (the BOSCC
    // gadget, "Which arms get a gadget" in SSA/Linearize.h). The linearizer
    // puts one there wherever the arm touches memory or calls -- which in this
    // render is every arm of the Shape, Primitive and Light dispatches, since
    // their variants are stored `tagged_index` and an arm begins by loading
    // its own -- and otherwise only where this block says so:
    //
    //     material_bxdf.skip(Material);   // every arm of that match
    //     bxdf_f.skip(BxDF.Dielectric);   // one arm
    //     path_step.skip();               // every arm the function has
    //
    // Nothing is said, because measured (PLAN.md, "What is next", item 7:
    // five scenes, depth 1 and 5, 64 spp) the arithmetic-only arms are not
    // worth a test: the shading dispatches' arms cost 3-4% on killeroo and
    // book at depth 5 when tested, because a scene has one material and the
    // test never bypasses anything, and putting a test in front of every arm
    // of the path loop moves the scenes by no more than the run-to-run noise.
    // The place to say otherwise is a scene whose lanes split across arms of
    // a match whose arms are pure arithmetic, and the way to know is to count
    // (item 7's list).

    // The pixels across the machine's cores, and their finalization too.
    render.bind(p, CPUThread);
    render.bind(q, CPUThread);
}
